<section class="chapter" id="internet">
<h1>What is JavaScript?</h1>
<p>
        JavaScript is a programming language used to make
        websites interactive and dynamic. It allows developers
        to respond to user actions, update content, validate
        forms, create animations, and build web applications.
    </p>
<p>
        JavaScript works together with HTML and CSS to create
        complete websites. HTML defines the content, CSS
        controls its appearance, and JavaScript adds behavior.
    </p>
<h2>A High-Level Definition</h2>
<p>
        JavaScript is one of the three fundamental technologies
        used in front-end web development.
    </p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>HTML:</strong> Creates the structure of a
            web page, including headings, paragraphs, images,
            buttons, and forms.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>CSS:</strong> Controls colors, fonts,
            spacing, positioning, and responsive layouts.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>JavaScript:</strong> Adds interactive
            functionality and allows page content to change.
        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Example: Interactive Button</h3>
<p>
        The following example changes a button's text when
        a user clicks it.
    </p>
<pre><code>&lt;button id="welcomeButton"&gt;
    Click Me
&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
const button = document.getElementById("welcomeButton");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    button.textContent = "Welcome to JavaScript!";
});
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
<p>
        The HTML creates the button, while JavaScript detects
        the click and updates the displayed text.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
     CHAPTER 2: JAVASCRIPT CAPABILITIES
========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="javascript-capabilities">
<h2>So What Can JavaScript Really Do?</h2>
<p>
        JavaScript provides programming features that allow
        developers to process information and create
        interactive experiences.
    </p>
<h3>Variables</h3>
<p>
        Variables store values that can be used throughout
        a program.
    </p>
<pre><code>const websiteName = "CodeVerse";
let totalBooks = 10;

console.log(websiteName);
console.log(totalBooks);</code></pre>
<h3>Working with Strings</h3>
<p>
        Strings represent text. JavaScript can combine,
        compare, and modify strings.
    </p>
<pre><code>const firstName = "Alex";
const message = "Welcome, " + firstName;

console.log(message);</code></pre>
<h3>Responding to Events</h3>
<p>
        Events are actions that occur in a web page,
        such as clicking a button, typing in a text field,
        or submitting a form.
    </p>
<pre><code>const button = document.querySelector("#saveButton");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    alert("Your changes have been saved!");
});</code></pre>
<h2>Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)</h2>
<p>
        APIs provide predefined functionality that developers
        can use without building every feature from scratch.
        JavaScript can work with browser APIs and external
        services.
    </p>
<h3>Browser APIs</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>DOM API:</strong> Accesses and changes
            HTML elements and their properties.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Geolocation API:</strong> Requests access
            to the user's geographical location.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Canvas API:</strong> Draws graphics,
            shapes, and animations.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>WebGL API:</strong> Supports advanced
            graphics rendering.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Audio and Video APIs:</strong> Control
            multimedia playback and related features.
        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Third-Party APIs</h3>
<p>
        Third-party APIs are provided by external services.
        They allow websites to integrate features such as
        maps, weather information, and social media content.
    </p>
<p>
        Examples include mapping services and APIs that
        retrieve information from other websites.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
     CHAPTER 3: JAVASCRIPT EXECUTION
========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="javascript-execution">
<h2>What Is JavaScript Doing on Your Page?</h2>
<p>
        When a browser opens a web page, it processes
        HTML, applies CSS, and executes JavaScript.
        JavaScript can then modify the page while the
        user interacts with it.
    </p>
<h3>Browser Security</h3>
<p>
        Browsers use security restrictions to help prevent
        websites from accessing private information or
        interfering with unrelated websites.
    </p>
<p>
        JavaScript generally operates within the security
        environment provided by the browser. Access to
        sensitive features may require user permission.
    </p>
<h3>JavaScript Running Order</h3>
<p>
        JavaScript statements generally execute in sequence.
        Variables and elements must be available before
        code attempts to use them.
    </p>
<pre><code>const username = "Alex";

console.log(username);

const greeting = "Hello, " + username;

console.log(greeting);</code></pre>
<p>
        In this example, the username is defined before
        it is used to create the greeting.
    </p>
<h3>Interpreted Versus Compiled Code</h3>
<p>
        Programming languages can use different methods
        to prepare and execute instructions.
    </p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Interpreted execution:</strong>
            Processes instructions during program execution.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Compiled execution:</strong>
            Transforms source code into another representation
            before it runs.
        </li>
</ul>
<p>
        Modern JavaScript engines commonly use
        just-in-time compilation to improve performance.
        Developers can still write JavaScript directly
        without manually compiling ordinary browser scripts.
    </p>
<h3>Client-Side Versus Server-Side JavaScript</h3>
<p>
        Client-side JavaScript runs in the user's browser.
        It is commonly responsible for interactive buttons,
        form behavior, and dynamic page updates.
    </p>
<p>
        Server-side JavaScript runs on a server, often using
        environments such as Node.js. It can process requests,
        interact with databases, and generate responses.
    </p>
<pre><code>// Client-side example
document.title = "CodeVerse Library";

// Server-side Node.js example
console.log("Server application started");</code></pre>
<p>
        Both approaches can work together to build
        complete web applications.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
     CHAPTER 4: ADDING JAVASCRIPT
========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="adding-javascript">
<h2>How Do You Add JavaScript to Your Page?</h2>
<p>
        JavaScript is connected to HTML using the
        &lt;script&gt; element. Scripts can be written
        directly inside HTML or stored in external files.
    </p>
<h3>Internal JavaScript</h3>
<p>
        Internal JavaScript is written inside a script
        element in the HTML document.
    </p>
<pre><code>&lt;button id="helloButton"&gt;
    Say Hello
&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
document.getElementById("helloButton")
    .addEventListener("click", function() {
        alert("Hello, CodeVerse!");
    });
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
<h3>External JavaScript</h3>
<p>
        External JavaScript is stored in a separate file
        with the .js extension. This makes code easier
        to organize and maintain.
    </p>
<p><strong>HTML:</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;script src="script.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
<p><strong>script.js:</strong></p>
<pre><code>console.log("External JavaScript loaded!");</code></pre>
<h3>Inline JavaScript</h3>
<p>
        JavaScript can also be written directly inside
        an HTML event attribute.
    </p>
<pre><code>&lt;button onclick="alert('Hello!')"&gt;
    Click Me
&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
<p>
        Although inline JavaScript works, using
        addEventListener() in a separate script is generally
        preferred because it keeps HTML and JavaScript
        responsibilities separate.
    </p>
<h3>Script Loading Strategies</h3>
<p>
        The position and attributes of a script element
        influence when JavaScript executes.
    </p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>defer:</strong> Downloads a classic
            external script while HTML is being parsed,
            then executes it after parsing is complete.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>async:</strong> Downloads a script
            without blocking parsing and executes it
            when available, without guaranteeing order
            relative to other scripts.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>type="module":</strong> Loads JavaScript
            as a module and defers execution by default.
        </li>
</ul>
<pre><code>&lt;script src="app.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;script src="analytics.js" async&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;script type="module" src="main.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
     CHAPTER 5: JAVASCRIPT COMMENTS
========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="javascript-comments">
<h2>Comments</h2>
<p>
        Comments are notes written inside source code
        to explain its purpose. JavaScript ignores comments
        when executing a program.
    </p>
<h3>Single-Line Comments</h3>
<p>
        A single-line comment begins with two forward
        slashes.
    </p>
<pre><code>// Display a message
console.log("Hello World");</code></pre>
<h3>Multi-Line Comments</h3>
<p>
        Multi-line comments begin with /* and end with */.
        They are useful for explanations spanning several lines.
    </p>
<pre><code>/*
    This function displays a welcome
    message in the browser console.
*/

function welcome() {
    console.log("Welcome to CodeVerse!");
}</code></pre>
<h3>Why Comments Are Important</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explain complicated code.</li>
<li>Help developers understand program behavior.</li>
<li>Improve teamwork and maintainability.</li>
<li>Document important decisions.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>
        JavaScript adds dynamic functionality to websites.
        It works alongside HTML and CSS, supports variables,
        events, and APIs, and can run in both browsers and
        server environments.
    </p>
<p>
        Developers can add JavaScript internally or through
        external files, control script loading, and use
        comments to make programs easier to understand.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
     CHAPTER 6: DOM SCRIPTING INTRODUCTION
========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="dom-scripting">
<h1>DOM Scripting Introduction</h1>
<p>
        DOM scripting is the process of using JavaScript
        to access and modify the structure, content,
        and appearance of a web page.
    </p>
<p>
        The Document Object Model, or DOM, provides
        a programming interface that represents an
        HTML document as a collection of connected objects.
    </p>
<h2>The Important Parts of a Web Browser</h2>
<p>
        JavaScript interacts with several browser objects
        that provide access to different features.
    </p>
<h3>The Window Object</h3>
<p>
        The window object represents the browser window
        or tab containing the web page.
    </p>
<pre><code>console.log(window.innerWidth);
console.log(window.innerHeight);</code></pre>
<p>
        These properties return the dimensions of the
        browser's viewport.
    </p>
<h3>The Navigator Object</h3>
<p>
        The navigator object provides information about
        the browser and selected capabilities.
    </p>
<pre><code>console.log(navigator.language);
console.log(navigator.onLine);</code></pre>
<h3>The Document Object</h3>
<p>
        The document object represents the web page
        and allows JavaScript to access its elements.
    </p>
<pre><code>console.log(document.title);

document.title = "Learning DOM Scripting";</code></pre>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
     CHAPTER 7: DOCUMENT OBJECT MODEL
========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="document-object-model">
<h2>The Document Object Model</h2>
<p>
        The DOM organizes an HTML document into a tree-like
        structure. Each element, text section, and other
        document component is represented by a node.
    </p>
<h3>Example HTML Structure</h3>
<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;body&gt;
        &lt;section&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;CodeVerse&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;Learn JavaScript&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;/section&gt;
    &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
<h3>DOM Tree Representation</h3>
<pre><code>HTML
 └── BODY
      └── SECTION
           ├── H1
           │    └── "CodeVerse"
           └── P
                └── "Learn JavaScript"</code></pre>
<h3>DOM Node Relationships</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Root Node:</strong> The topmost
            element of the document tree.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Parent Node:</strong> A node that
            contains another node.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Child Node:</strong> A node directly
            contained within another node.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Sibling Nodes:</strong> Nodes that
            share the same parent.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Descendant Node:</strong> A node
            contained anywhere inside another node,
            even several levels deeper.
        </li>
</ul>
<p>
        Understanding these relationships helps developers
        navigate and manipulate HTML elements.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
     CHAPTER 8: BASIC DOM MANIPULATION
========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="dom-manipulation">
<h2>Doing Some Basic DOM Manipulation</h2>
<p>
        DOM manipulation allows JavaScript to select
        elements, change their content, create new
        elements, remove existing elements, and
        modify styles.
    </p>
<h3>Selecting an Element</h3>
<p>
        The querySelector() method returns the first
        element matching a CSS selector.
    </p>
<pre><code>const heading = document.querySelector("h1");

console.log(heading);</code></pre>
<h3>Selecting Multiple Elements</h3>
<p>
        The querySelectorAll() method returns all
        matching elements as a NodeList.
    </p>
<pre><code>const paragraphs = document.querySelectorAll("p");

paragraphs.forEach(function(paragraph) {
    console.log(paragraph.textContent);
});</code></pre>
<h3>Other Selection Methods</h3>
<pre><code>// Select by ID
const title = document.getElementById("pageTitle");

// Select by tag name
const items = document.getElementsByTagName("li");</code></pre>
<h3>Changing Text Content</h3>
<p>
        The textContent property allows developers
        to read or replace the text inside an element.
    </p>
<pre><code>const title = document.getElementById("pageTitle");

title.textContent = "Welcome to CodeVerse";</code></pre>
<h3>Changing Attributes</h3>
<p>
        JavaScript can update attributes such as
        href, src, alt, and class.
    </p>
<pre><code>const link = document.querySelector("#resourceLink");

link.href = "https://developer.mozilla.org/";
link.textContent = "Visit MDN";</code></pre>
<h3>Creating New Elements</h3>
<p>
        The createElement() method creates an HTML
        element that can be inserted into the page.
    </p>
<pre><code>const paragraph = document.createElement("p");

paragraph.textContent = "This is a new paragraph.";

document.body.appendChild(paragraph);</code></pre>
<h3>Creating Text Nodes</h3>
<p>
        Text nodes can be created separately and
        attached to existing elements.
    </p>
<pre><code>const message = document.createElement("p");

const text = document.createTextNode(
    "JavaScript can create text dynamically."
);

message.appendChild(text);

document.body.appendChild(message);</code></pre>
<h3>Removing Elements</h3>
<p>
        The remove() method deletes an element
        from the DOM.
    </p>
<pre><code>const message = document.querySelector("#oldMessage");

if (message) {
    message.remove();
}</code></pre>
<h3>Moving Elements</h3>
<p>
        Appending an existing DOM element to a different
        parent moves that element to its new position.
    </p>
<pre><code>const item = document.querySelector("#bookItem");
const container = document.querySelector("#bookContainer");

if (item &amp;&amp; container) {
    container.appendChild(item);
}</code></pre>
<h3>Manipulating CSS Styles</h3>
<p>
        JavaScript can change inline styles through
        an element's style property.
    </p>
<pre><code>const heading = document.querySelector("#pageTitle");

heading.style.color = "blue";
heading.style.fontSize = "30px";
heading.style.backgroundColor = "lightgray";</code></pre>
<h3>Using CSS Classes</h3>
