<section class="chapter" id="dynamic-shopping-list">
<h2>Creating a Dynamic Shopping List</h2>
<p>
        A dynamic shopping list demonstrates how
        JavaScript can create and remove HTML elements
        in response to user input.
    </p>
<h3>Learning Objectives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Read text from an input field.</li>
<li>Create new list items.</li>
<li>Display items on the page.</li>
<li>Remove items using buttons.</li>
<li>Clear and refocus the input field.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Interactive Example</h3>
<form id="cvShoppingForm">
<label for="cvShoppingInput">
            Enter a shopping item:
        </label>
<input id="cvShoppingInput" placeholder="Example: Notebook" required="" type="text"/>
<button type="submit">
            Add Item
        </button>
</form>
<ul id="cvShoppingList"></ul>
<h3>JavaScript Implementation</h3>
<p>
        The following script selects the form and list,
        creates new elements when an item is submitted,
        and removes items when their Delete buttons
        are clicked.
    </p>
<pre><code>const form = document.getElementById("cvShoppingForm");
const input = document.getElementById("cvShoppingInput");
const list = document.getElementById("cvShoppingList");

form.addEventListener("submit", function(event) {

    event.preventDefault();

    const itemName = input.value.trim();

    if (itemName === "") {
        return;
    }

    const listItem = document.createElement("li");
    const itemText = document.createElement("span");
    const deleteButton = document.createElement("button");

    itemText.textContent = itemName;
    deleteButton.textContent = "Delete";
    deleteButton.type = "button";

    listItem.appendChild(itemText);
    listItem.appendChild(deleteButton);

    list.appendChild(listItem);

    deleteButton.addEventListener("click", function() {
        listItem.remove();
    });

    input.value = "";
    input.focus();

});</code></pre>
<h3>How the Program Works</h3>
<ol>
<li>
            The user enters the name of an item.
        </li>
<li>
            The form's submit event is detected.
        </li>
<li>
            preventDefault() stops the page from reloading.
        </li>
<li>
            JavaScript creates a list item and a Delete button.
        </li>
<li>
            The new item is appended to the shopping list.
        </li>
<li>
            Clicking Delete removes that item.
        </li>
<li>
            The input is cleared and focused for the next entry.
        </li>
</ol>
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>
        DOM scripting allows developers to control web page
        content using JavaScript. The DOM represents HTML
        as a tree of connected nodes that can be selected,
        created, modified, moved, and removed.
    </p>
<p>
        By combining DOM methods with event listeners,
        developers can build interactive features such
        as shopping lists, navigation menus, form validation,
        and dynamic book libraries.
    </p>
